# Ploaness Agent Guide

Read `README-guideline-software-project.adoc`, this guide, and then the project-owned instructions in
`AGENTS.md`. Each layer may strengthen the earlier contract. The guideline states the general rules;
this guide supplies the Ploaness-specific workflow, settings, and responsibilities.

## Ownership and Requirements

Ploaness governs Git repositories containing at least one Payload CMS application. A repository can
also contain Next.js applications and libraries. Member kinds are derived from their dependencies:
`payload` identifies a Payload application, `next` identifies another application, and neither identifies
a library. A project cannot select a weaker kind through configuration.

Use Node 26 or later and the exact pnpm version Ploaness pins. Declare `ploaness` as a devDependency.
Every application also declares `@ploaness/runtime` in dependencies at the same release version.
Import harness configurations and test helpers through `ploaness`; import production helpers through
`@ploaness/runtime`. Library members do not need the runtime package.

Ploaness owns analyzer versions, configurations, gate selection, thresholds, and managed content.
Consumer settings describe the project or tighten an allowed threshold. Do not replace the harness,
copy its rules locally, or suppress a finding where a structural repair is available.

Container analyzers and the browser suite require Docker; the browser suite also requires the Playwright
image pulled beforehand. Dependency and vulnerability checks require their registries.
Unavailable infrastructure and tool failures fail verification. Set up the required infrastructure and
rerun; an incomplete run establishes no full verdict.

### Repository and Member Configuration

Run full verification from the repository root. Repository gates run once, package gates run per governed
member, and Payload gates run only for Payload members. Registry order determines the sequence; a
workspace report labels each member invocation. Libraries receive the framework-neutral configurations
and have no application build or browser suite.

Workspace package patterns support plain directory names, `*`, `?`, `**`, and explicit brace
alternatives such as `packages/{ui,shared}`. Plain names use letters, numbers, spaces, `_`, `-`, `.`,
`@`, and `!`. Brackets, quotes, backslashes, other special syntax, adjacent `**` segments, ranges,
empty alternatives, and singleton or literal brace groups fail with a diagnostic. Spell out supported
alternatives and use separate leading-`!` exclusion patterns instead.

Ploaness requires the root verification scripts and each member's inherited configuration:

- `biome.json` extends only `ploaness/biome` for applications or `ploaness/biome-core` for libraries.
  Local overrides, language rules, formatting, plugins, and VCS selection are harness-owned. Its
  member-relative file-selection block is governed; nested configurations also carry `root: false`.
- `tsconfig.json` extends `ploaness/tsconfig.json` or `ploaness/tsconfig-core.json`. Only the project
  paths and compiler options the wiring gate permits may be added.
- `eslint.config.mjs` and `vitest.config.mts` contain only the appropriate Ploaness import and default
  re-export. The test gate selects `vitest.config.mts` explicitly. Libraries use `ploaness/eslint-library`
  and `ploaness/vitest-library`.
- Applications also re-export `ploaness/playwright` from `playwright.config.ts`.

The workspace install policy belongs in the root `pnpm-workspace.yaml`: declare the install-script
allowlist and `minimumReleaseAgeStrict: true`. Release-age exclusions may name only `ploaness` and
`@ploaness/*`. The wiring gate reports required dependency pins and rejects overrides of harness-owned
versions. A permitted override may repair an unpinned transitive dependency.

### Managed Files

The managed editor settings use spaces, LF endings, UTF-8 without a BOM, and a 120-character line
width. Indentation is four spaces generally and two spaces for YAML, JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON,
and JSONC. The inherited Biome formatter uses the same widths for the languages it formats.
The EditorConfig gate retains the code-role line cap and generated-file exceptions.

`ploaness sync` applies the managed catalogue and generated-file write denials. The catalogue distinguishes:

- **Pinned:** content is owned by Ploaness and must match its shipped bytes.
- **Section:** Ploaness owns the leading marked block of `AGENTS.md`; project instructions follow it.
- **Seed:** written only when absent, then owned by the project, such as the initial proxy and ignore file.
- **Reference:** an optional agent-specific instruction file may contain only a reference to `AGENTS.md`.
- **Forbidden:** a local analyzer configuration that would shadow the harness may not exist. Neither
  may `CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, or `.claude/CLAUDE.md` in any directory: Claude Code reads `AGENTS.md`.

Repair pinned drift with `ploaness sync`, not a hand edit. Malformed or duplicate managed markers require
manual repair before synchronization. Generated application files change through their generator.
`unmanagedAssets` requires an explicit assignment to take ownership and a recorded reason.

## Workflow

1. Inspect the repository instructions and current changes before editing.
2. For initial setup, install `ploaness`, run `pnpm exec ploaness init`, satisfy the dependency and
   workspace requirements reported by wiring, and run `pnpm install`. Init updates the root scripts,
   seeds missing configuration files, and synchronizes managed content; it leaves existing member
   configurations in place. `sync` repairs managed files, not project-owned configuration stubs.
3. Implement the change and its meaningful tests. Run `pnpm run format` to apply Biome and ESLint fixes.
4. Use `pnpm run verify` (quick verification) during development. Use
   `pnpm exec ploaness gate <id> --verbose` to diagnose an individual gate; its analyzer output is
   evidence for that gate alone.
5. Run `pnpm run verify:delta` before finishing. It adds history, production build, bundle, and the
   browser suite, with the browser sweep scoped to the routes and stories the change reaches; it writes
   the dependency inventory and the compliance report, and must reach the final tree check without a
   failure. `pnpm run verify:full` sweeps every route and story in every engine. It belongs to CI,
   nightly on the default branch and on each release commit; run it locally only to reproduce a CI
   failure.
6. Review the diff, commit, and verify the resulting commit. A commit changing more than two files or
   more than 50 added/deleted lines needs a body explaining why. Check a new message with
   `pnpm exec ploaness commit-message --range HEAD~1..HEAD`; the complete policy is in the guideline.

Verification stops at its first failing gate. Fix the reported cause, then start a new full run.
A formatter or generator that changes source during verification leaves the tree unverified until the
changes are reviewed and a subsequent run leaves it unchanged. Logs and build artifacts belong in
ignored locations. Do not edit source while a full run is in progress.

`--enforce=false` surveys findings and exits zero; that exit status is not a pass. Failed preconditions
still stop the survey. `ploaness gates` lists the current registry with the level that first runs each
gate; `--scope=repository --ids` restricts that listing. Neither a diagnostic subset nor a survey
replaces delta verification.

When a correct program is blocked by a reproducible harness defect, repair it in Ploaness. Preserve the
failing output and a minimal synthetic reproduction. A consumer must not weaken a check to hide a
harness bug. A managed sweep can contain a defect just as another check can.

## Gates

There are three levels, and each contains the one before it. Quick verification (`ploaness verify`,
`pnpm run verify`) runs 38 gates. These are gate definitions; workspace members can produce multiple
invocations of a package gate. `tree-verify` runs after all other gates at every level.

### Quick Verification Gates

| Gate | Scope | What it verifies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `preflight` | repository | The repository meets the harness requirements and holds a Payload member. |
| `wiring` | repository | The scripts, the five project files, and the pinned versions are intact and nothing undoes them. |
| `assets` | repository | Every managed file matches what ploaness ships. |
| `tree-snapshot` | repository | Records a fingerprint of the working tree. |
| `types` | package | Strict TypeScript checking for the member. |
| `biome` | package | Formatting and the fast lint pass. |
| `biome-schema` | repository | The `$schema` in `biome.json` names the installed Biome version. |
| `oxlint` | package | Three native rules across authored JS/TS plus 31 application JSX accessibility checks. Libraries retain Biome accessibility. |
| `eslint` | package | The remaining type-aware and specialized lint checks. |
| `conventions` | repository | The typography ban and the hand-written JavaScript ban, over tracked and non-ignored untracked text files, and documenting comments left above another comment rather than above what they document. |
| `editorconfig` | repository | Every tracked and non-ignored untracked text file matches the pinned `.editorconfig`, and no code line passes 120 characters. |
| `suppressions` | package | The suppression count against the earned ceiling, over the project's own files. |
| `css` | package | Stylelint over authored CSS and SCSS under the source roots, plus SCSS formatting. Tailwind v4 is accepted as written. |
| `tailwind-tokens` | package | Every visual value in the markup comes from the theme rather than an arbitrary value. |
| `arch` | package | dependency-cruiser: layering, cycles, and orphans. |
| `type-coverage` | package | 100% type coverage of authored production TypeScript; test assertions are excluded from this measurement. |
| `payload-generated` | payload | The generated Payload types and admin import map are current. |
| `payload-defaults` | payload | Resolved production access decisions, draft protection, positive native authentication limits, and disabled admin auto-login. |
| `generated-denial` | repository | The agent runtime is denied write access to every generated artefact. |
| `payload-rules` | package | Source policy, including explicit Payload options, access control, request threading, drafts, upload restrictions, reviewable schema migrations, and boot-time writes. |
| `admin-views` | payload | Static evidence that custom admin views have project-owned accessibility specs; the browser suite establishes execution. |
| `config-refs` | package | No analyzer setting carves out a path that no longer exists or matches nothing. |
| `secrets` | repository | gitleaks over the git history, plus the declared `secretAllowlist`. |
| `licenses` | repository | The license of every dependency sits inside the allowlist. |
| `vulnerabilities` | repository | Every advisory on a resolved package, whatever its severity, is repaired or has a recorded mitigation. |
| `install-scripts` | repository | The allowlist naming which dependencies may run a build or postinstall script. |
| `release-age` | repository | pnpm's release-age floor is strict, and only the ploaness packages are excluded from it. |
| `blocklist` | repository | No container image, Dockerfile system package, or installed npm package is one ploaness refuses by name, and every image reference is pinned to a tag. |
| `deps` | repository | No declared dependency is two or more major versions behind. Pinned images are reported, never failed. |
| `image-assets` | repository | Every working-tree image decodes as the format its bytes claim. |
| `environment` | repository | Every variable the application reads, and every one a compose file interpolates, is documented in the example file and supplied by each verifying workflow, and every `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variable a member inlines at build time is declared as an `ARG` in the Dockerfile that builds it. |
| `docs` | repository | The agent documentation names no script and no file that has gone. |
| `skills` | repository | The metadata header of every `SKILL.md`. |
| `docker` | repository | hadolint over every Dockerfile, and `docker compose config`. |
| `actions` | repository | actionlint over every workflow. |
| `knip` | package | Dead code, unused files, and unused dependencies. |
| `tests` | package | The suite and its per-file coverage floors, under the network guard and the shuffle. Asynchronous assertions must be awaited. |
| `tree-verify` | repository | The working tree is unchanged since `tree-snapshot` ran. |

### Delta Verification Gates

Delta verification (`ploaness verify delta`, `pnpm run verify:delta`) adds 10 gates before the final
tree check. Full verification (`ploaness verify full`, `pnpm run verify:full`) runs the same gates; what
it adds is the complete browser sweep, every route and story in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. Outside
CI it first states that cost.

| Gate | Scope | What it verifies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `shell` | repository | ShellCheck over every tracked shell script, found by extension and by shebang. |
| `infra` | repository | Forbidden patterns over `.tf` and `.tfvars`, plus curated Checkov checks for AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, DigitalOcean and Linode; a provider the harness has not classified fails. Each `aws_cloudfront_function` must deploy a tracked file allowlisted for its runtime. |
| `require-full-history` | repository | The clone carries the whole history. |
| `commit-history` | repository | Every commit message in the history. |
| `linear-history` | repository | No merge commit anywhere in the history. |
| `build` | package | The production build. |
| `bundle` | package | The gzipped client JavaScript in Next's configured production output stays under the bundle budget. |
| `e2e` | package | The end-to-end suite in the pinned browsers, including the three sweeps ploaness ships into it. At delta the browser sweep covers what the change reaches, in Chromium; at full it runs every pass in Chromium over everything and its engine pass in Firefox and WebKit. |
| `sbom` | repository | Generates and validates the resolved workspace dependency inventory and its source metadata. |
| `compliance-report` | repository | Writes the shareable compliance report from the inventory `sbom` wrote for the current tree. |

The `infra` gate reads the providers a tree declares from its `resource`, `data` and `provider`
headers and judges only what it can see: resources inside external modules are not downloaded. A
provider with curated checks is analysed. A utility provider such as `random` is ignored. A provider
Checkov ships no check for passes on the pattern rules, and the summary says so. A provider the harness
has not classified fails until Ploaness classifies it. The enabled checks refuse defects only, never
operational policy, and there is no per-check opt-out; which checks are enabled, and why each one is,
is recorded in Ploaness beside its identifier. Two AWS checks are deliberately left off because they
also refuse the correct form: the plain-HTTP listener check fails a load balancer behind a CDN, and the
SSH and RDP checks read a rule that names another security group as its source as open to the world.
Every port from the world is still refused by the analyzer, and a standalone
`aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule` that admits every port, SSH or RDP from every address is refused
by a pattern rule, which reads that one resource because its type names its direction. Two more
pattern rules close what the analyzer leaves open on every cloud: an outright administrative grant
(`roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `Owner`) and an explicit plaintext-transport setting.

Each `aws_cloudfront_function` must declare `runtime = "cloudfront-js-2.0"` and load its code with
`file("${path.module}/<path>")`. That path must name a tracked file whose `javascriptAllowlist` entry
declares the same runtime. Other `code` forms fail, because the gate cannot tell which file is
deployed: `templatefile`, a variable, a heredoc, or a path relative to where OpenTofu runs. A build
output the repository does not track fails too. The `cloudfront-js-2.0` lint rules follow AWS's
documented feature list. They reject destructuring, object spread, and the globals the runtime lacks,
such as `Set`, `Map` and `process`. Passing them lowers the risk without proving the function runs: only
`aws cloudfront test-function` does, and it exits successfully even when the function fails, so read
`FunctionErrorMessage` in its result. A function deployed by anything other than these `.tf` files is
linted only for the runtime its entry declares.

## Release Dependency Inventory

Full verification runs the required `sbom` gate once for the repository, after the browser suite and before
the final tree check. It writes bom.cdx.json and source metadata in release.json under the repository's ignored
dist/sbom directory. The metadata contains no release artifact associations at this stage; full verification does
not assume a container or archive format. A generation failure fails verification, even if an older inventory exists.

Run `ploaness sbom --artifact <file> [--artifact <file> ...] [--output <directory>]` after building
the release files. The command is repository-scoped and accepts local archive files, including an exported
container-image archive. Artifact filenames must be distinct. It generates CycloneDX JSON 1.6 and a release
metadata sidecar in the ignored dist/sbom directory by default. Paths supplied on the command line are relative
to the current directory. A custom output directory must be ignored by Git or outside the repository.

The workspace must already be installed with the pinned pnpm, and its manifests, lockfile, and installed lock
snapshot must agree. The digest-pinned cdxgen image reads a bounded copy of workspace manifests and lockfiles.
Generation runs without networking, dependency installation, lifecycle scripts, or source-usage filtering.
Docker may need network access to acquire that image before generation. A failed generator or incomplete report
fails the command. No advisory service is involved. Each component then records the license its installed manifest
declares, read from the same workspace-wide pnpm inventory the `licenses` gate judges: an SPDX expression where the
declared text is one, otherwise a named license. The component scope is the generator's own.

Retain both output files with the release. Metadata records the source commit and dirty state, source fingerprint,
lockfile hash, generator identity, SBOM hash, and SHA-256 hashes of the release files. A dirty checkout is identified
as such; it is not represented as the committed source. Ploaness's own release workflow requires a clean source
commit and retains the reports before publishing, then attaches them to the GitHub Release. Recovery requires the
original reports and verifies them against the original commit and published archive bytes.

This inventory covers resolved workspace dependencies, including development tools. Per-workspace direct dependency
roles are recorded in its metadata; transitive runtime usage is not inferred from import syntax. Bundling may remove
listed packages, and container OS packages are outside this inventory. SBOM generation neither replaces the
vulnerability gate nor proves deployment provenance.

## Compliance Report

Full verification runs the `compliance-report` gate after `sbom`. It writes one self-contained HTML document
under the repository's ignored dist/compliance directory, named after the project and the source commit, and a
PDF rendering of the same document beside it. That pair is what a project sends when a reviewer asks for a
license and vulnerability report on its open source dependencies. Earlier reports in the directory are removed,
so it always holds the report for the current tree.

The first page states the verdict as checked or failed statements, without calling the project compliant, and
lists the contents. The report then lists every vulnerability the `vulnerabilities` gate read with its scope and
status, open or mitigated, and prints a mitigation plan for each mitigated one from its allowlist entry. It
follows with the license classes, every weak copyleft or refused package with its scope, the license policy the
`licenses` gate enforces, the full package inventory, and the license and notice files the production
dependencies ship. It embeds the software bill of materials the `sbom` gate wrote, byte for byte, with its hash.
The gate fails when that inventory doesn't describe the current tree, when the license inventory or the advisory
database can't be read, or when the tree changes while the report is written. A dirty tree or a dependency
outside policy marks the report as a draft.

Scope is production when a version is reachable from a production dependency of any workspace package, and
development otherwise. The vulnerability section reflects the advisory database at the time of the query, so it
can change for the same source. The PDF is printed by the Chromium of the project's own `@playwright/test`,
which the `e2e` gate already requires. A repository where no workspace package declares it gets the HTML alone.
Run `ploaness compliance-report [--output <directory>]` to regenerate the inventory and the report outside full
verification. A custom output directory must be ignored by Git or outside the repository.

## Vulnerability Mitigations

The `vulnerabilities` gate judges every advisory, whatever its severity. An advisory is open, which fails the gate,
until the package is upgraded past it or an entry in `ploaness.vulnerabilityAllowlist` mitigates it. Upgrade
whenever a release, or a pnpm override within the dependent's supported range, removes the vulnerable version.
Record an entry only when none does. Every field is required, and an incomplete entry is ignored, so the advisory
stays open:

- `advisory`: the advisory identifier, such as a GHSA or CVE identifier.
- `reason`: why the vulnerability can't be exploited in this repository. Name the path that reaches the package,
  whether it runs in production or only in development and build, and why its input can't trigger the defect.
- `mitigation`: the mitigation plan. State what limits the risk now and what will remove the package, such as
  the upstream release or the dependent's upgrade it waits for.
- `addedOn`: the date the entry was recorded, as `YYYY-MM-DD`.

```json
{
  "advisory": "GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx",
  "reason": "example-parser is reached only through example-bundler, which parses the repository's own build configuration during development and build. The defect needs attacker-supplied input, which that configuration isn't.",
  "mitigation": "example-bundler never runs in the deployed application. The entry is removed once example-bundler releases a version that drops example-parser.",
  "addedOn": "2026-01-15"
}
```

The compliance report prints the reason and the mitigation plan to a reviewer outside the project, so write both
as complete sentences that stand on their own. An entry that matches no current advisory fails the gate and must
be removed.

## Consumer Settings

Typography and EditorConfig exclude binary assets by content, without project-specific path exclusions.
Their shared detector checks the complete file for NUL bytes and invalid UTF-8, and recognises PDF
headers even when a PDF uses only text-encoded objects. Valid UTF-8 text remains in scope regardless
of its extension, including SVG and JSON files.

Settings live under `ploaness` in `package.json`. A member inherits the root settings. Filesystem patterns and directory declarations retain their
declaring directory: root declarations are repository-relative, and member declarations are member-relative.
Use an explicit cross-member glob such as `**/scripts/deploy.ts` to reach matching paths in every member.
The built-in source and role defaults apply separately to each member. Source and role lists add entries; command arrays and origins describe one member and replace inherited values.
Numeric ceilings honor the smallest valid value and cannot weaken an ancestor; the layout gap is a
floor and honors the largest. Environment maps merge
by variable name. Malformed values use the strict default or contribute no exception; a malformed
member ceiling cannot discard a valid root ceiling.

File-role declarations use `{ "pattern": "...", "reason": "..." }`. The reason names the file's role,
not the convenience of excluding it. Patterns must reach files the relevant check would otherwise judge.
Regexes and globs are identified below; a member's regex applies to paths inside that member. Route
prefixes name URLs and are not checked against filesystem paths.

Generated-file roles are excluded from formatting, lint, type coverage, and test coverage in every member kind.
Coverage includes eligible `.ts`, `.mts`, and `.cts` source under every source root; existing test,
declaration, view, and framework roles remain excluded. Glob roles support comma-separated brace alternatives,
such as `src/{read,write}.ts`. Each explicit exclusion must reach an eligible file.

`ploaness format` formats SCSS with the harness's pinned Prettier settings. Stylelint owns SCSS rules;
Biome owns formatting for its supported languages. `.cjs` is parsed as CommonJS and may initialize its
exports, while ordinary mutations remain prohibited in every directory.

| Setting | Default | Input and effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sourceRoots` | `src`, `tests`, `scripts` | String array adding source directories, including coverage of `.ts`, `.mts`, and `.cts` logic. |
| `unmanagedAssets` | empty | Entries with `path` and `reason` recording explicitly assigned project ownership. |
| `typographyExclusions` | generated Payload files | File-role entries with regex patterns. |
| `javascriptAllowlist` | ESLint/PostCSS configs and generated import map | Entries with `pattern`, `reason`, and `runtime` for necessary `.js`, `.jsx`, `.mjs`, or `.cjs` files; never TypeScript. `runtime` is required and is `node` or `cloudfront-js-2.0`; an entry without a valid one is not honored. Matching authored files are linted for that runtime, formatted, and passed to knip as entry points. |
| `coverageExclude` | framework glue, generated files, components | File-role entries with glob patterns; each must reach measured code. |
| `frameworkGlue` | `src/app/(payload)/**`, `src/payload.config.ts` | File-role entries with globs for framework scaffolding; authored behavior is not scaffolding. |
| `generatedArtefacts` | stock Payload types, import map, schema | File-role entries with globs for generator-owned files, added to the shipped set. |
| `pureLogicRoots` | `src/access`, `src/lib` | File-role entries naming additional pure-logic directories. |
| `bundleBudgetBytes` | `1126400` | Positive integer; only a smaller budget is honored. |
| `maxSuppressions` | earned ceiling | Nonnegative integer setting a stricter ceiling; zero permits none. |
| `vulnerabilityAllowlist` | empty | Root only. Mitigations for advisories no upgrade repairs; see Vulnerability Mitigations. Unused entries fail. |
| `secretAllowlist` | empty | Root only. Entries with repository-relative `path` and `reason` for committed fake credentials. |
| `serverUrl` | `http://localhost:3000` | Origin of the member's application under test. |
| `auxiliaryServers` | empty | Entries with `command`, `url`, and optional member-relative `cwd`; starts additional application dependencies for browser tests. |
| `publicAccess` | empty | Entries with `entity`, `operation`, `reason`, and optional `fields`, declaring intentional anonymous permissions. |
| `accessibilitySkipRoutes` | `/admin`, `/api` | Built-ins match exact paths and child paths. File-role-shaped entries add URL prefixes; explain why each is outside the sweep. |
| `accessibilityRouteBudget` | `250` | Positive integer; a smaller ceiling is honored. Reaching the ceiling fails rather than truncating coverage. |
| `layoutMinimumGap` | `4` | Positive integer in CSS pixels; only a larger gap is honored. |
| `layoutViewports` | empty | Entries with positive integer `width` and `height`, measured beside the baseline viewports; none can be removed. |
| `clock` | `2100-01-01T12:00:00Z` | ISO instant the application and browser clocks start at during verification, or `"real"`. Any other value is the default. |
| `analysisEnv` | placeholders for `PAYLOAD_SECRET`, `DATABASE_URL` | String-valued map used by analysis gates that import the application. |
| `pretest` | empty | Argument array for preparation before tests, builds, bundle inspection, and end-to-end tests. |
| `testWrapper` | empty | Argument array wrapping those processes, including the bundle configuration probe, for example to supply a disposable database. |

`publicAccess.fields` lists exact field paths such as `sessions.id`. `*` names the map Payload reports
when every field is open. A suffix such as `sizes.**` declares that group and all descendants, including
future fields; other wildcard positions are literal. Missing fields remain undeclared, and permissions
or fields the application does not grant make the corresponding declaration stale.

Runtime tests use the real environment. Vitest and direct local Playwright runs load `.env.local`
followed by `.env` without replacing already-set variables, including empty strings. Production browser
verification uses the installed Next environment loader before importing specs, retaining production-file
precedence, variable expansion, and supplied process values as the production build does.
`analysisEnv` fills only variables missing from both the process and `.env.local`/`.env`; it is for
import-only analysis, not a substitute for
the database and services that runtime verification starts. Keep environment reads in
`src/lib/environment.ts`, validate them there, and document required variables in the example file.

## Application Code and Tests

The `payload-defaults` probe selects the production configuration with `NODE_ENV=production`, overriding
any conflicting analysis setting. It checks resolved values after plugins and defaults. Native authentication
requires a positive integer attempt limit, a finite positive lock time in milliseconds, and a finite positive
token lifetime in seconds. Disabled local strategies are outside that native limit check. Production
`admin.autoLogin` must be absent or false, including credential-prefill mode. The report never includes the
contents of the auto-login object. These checks inspect the supplied analysis configuration, not live identity
providers or deployed service settings.

The architecture floor is `src/access` and `src/lib`, plus declared `pureLogicRoots`; these directories
must not import higher application layers. `src/app` composes those layers. Collection, global, field,
and block definitions are declarative: import access rules, hooks, and validators by reference.

A collection declares access for create, read, update, and delete. A literal that copies a configuration,
such as `{ ...collection, fields }`, keeps that configuration's access when `collection` is declared in
the same file as a `CollectionConfig` and the copy writes no `access` of its own.

A plugin can add a field after the collection is defined and replace any declaration of it, as a storage
adapter does with the object key it stores. Access written on the collection does not reach that field.
Close it with a plugin listed after the one that adds it, which sets the field's `access` on the finished
configuration. The access boundary sweep reports such a field when an anonymous caller can read it.

At recognized Payload Local API call sites, use an explicit options object. A read states the depth or
limit its rule requires; a request-scoped call passes `req` to join the request's transaction. Calls
carrying `user`, calls composed with top-level spreads, and calls in endpoint modules or authored Next.js
route handlers explicitly state `overrideAccess: false`. Protected fields follow any spread that could
replace them. A nested spread in `data` is ordinary application data and does not replace call options.

The source checks recognize documented Payload receiver forms, such as `payload`, `req.payload`, and
`this.payload`; they are not a general data-flow proof of arbitrary aliases. Keep policy-sensitive calls
in the supported explicit form. A green static gate does not replace tests of the intended access boundary.

Vitest collects:

- `tests/unit/**/*.unit.spec.ts` for pure behavior;
- `tests/component/**/*.component.spec.ts` and `tests/component/**/*.component.spec.tsx` for components under jsdom;
- `tests/int/**/*.int.spec.ts` and `tests/int/**/*.int.spec.tsx` for integration behavior.

The `e2e` gate always starts fresh application and auxiliary servers, locally as well as in CI.
Delta and full verification test the production build already produced in that run. Running
`ploaness gate e2e` builds first and runs at the delta level. Ordinary Next output uses `next start`; standalone output uses its generated server with
the public and static assets prepared under generated output. Bundle measurement resolves the same
configured build directory and fails when that output is missing. Static exports and custom server layouts
are unsupported and fail explicitly. The normal pretest and test wrapper still supply isolated services.
Before running `ploaness verify delta`, `ploaness verify full` or `ploaness gate e2e`, stop servers
already listening at the configured `serverUrl` and `auxiliaryServers` addresses. An occupied port fails
the gate even when its health endpoint is failing. Ploaness reports the address to release and leaves
the existing process running. Direct local `pnpm exec playwright test` runs use development mode and
may reuse existing servers; they do not establish a verification verdict.

The `e2e` gate measures in the browsers of the Playwright image matching the project's resolved
Playwright, `mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v<version>-noble`, started in Docker for the run and removed
after it. The image is never pulled during verification; pull it beforehand with the command the gate
prints. On Linux the container shares the host network and reaches the application's loopback origins
directly. Docker Desktop does not share the host's loopback, so on macOS and Windows every request a
page makes travels through Playwright's tunnel, which makes each load several times slower; expect a
local run there to take longer than the same run in Linux CI.

Serve Storybook to the sweep as a static build: an auxiliary server such as
`storybook build -o .storybook-static --quiet && vite preview --outDir .storybook-static --port 6106 --strictPort`
loads each story in a fraction of the time `storybook dev` takes to compile it, and judges the stories a
release would ship. The application stays on the host. Under verification the build, the pretest command, the suite
runner and every application server start their clock at `ploaness.clock`, which then advances normally,
and the browser pages of the sweep read the same fixed instant. Declare `"clock": "real"` only when
sign-in depends on an external service validating real time, and cover date-dependent content with
stories or project tests instead. The suite never retries: a verdict that passes only on a retry is not
reproducible.

Playwright collects end-to-end specs under `tests/e2e`. Place tests in the collected paths. Test code
receives strict typing and linting; type assertions are excluded from the production type-coverage
measurement and expected numeric values need no named constants. Coverage floors still apply to each
measured production file.

Ordinary TypeScript helpers under `src/app/` are measured just like helpers under `src/lib/`.
The App Router exclusion covers framework entry filenames, including pages, layouts, route handlers,
and metadata routes; it does not cover their whole directories. Keep authored business decisions in
measured modules and exercise framework entry points through the real request pipeline.
Authored `.tsx` and `.jsx` files must contain JSX syntax. Rename a logic-only `.tsx` module to `.ts`
so it retains its testing obligation. Generated-file roles remain excluded from the native filename rule.
Payload's default admin `page.tsx` and `not-found.tsx` scaffolds may retain their upstream filenames
when they delegate rendering without JSX. Their other native checks still apply; neighboring modules
and members without Payload retain the ordinary filename rule.
Oxlint reports a filename violation at file scope; a line suppression cannot hide it. Rename the file.

The network guard confines runtime tests to the local machine; subprocesses,
workers, and raw datagrams cannot bypass it through test code. Seed fast-check in the project's own
Vitest setup so it configures the module instance the suite actually imports.

Apply the guideline's behavioral test requirements as well as its automated checks: tests must judge
the behavior they name, reach meaningful assertions, and use real components. For cookie-authenticated
Payload requests with a CSRF allowlist, send the appropriate `Origin` header and assert the authenticated
outcome, so an anonymous response cannot accidentally satisfy the test.

### Browser Coverage and Security

Ploaness pins three executable sweeps into applications where they apply:

- The browser sweep discovers every public route reachable from home, every same-origin `hreflang`
  alternate, every page `sitemap.xml` lists, and every story an auxiliary server's `/index.json` lists,
  and judges each by the browser rules below. It excludes `/admin`, `/api`, and their child paths;
  project-owned custom admin views need their own authenticated scans, scoped to the authored view.
- Security headers checks the home response and HTML pages reached by the public crawl for content-type protection, referrer policy, a framing
  decision, and a script-constraining CSP. The seeded `src/proxy.ts` supplies a starting policy with a
  per-request nonce. Next stamps that nonce only on a page it renders per request: a page prerendered
  at build time loads no script under the policy, never hydrates, and the browser sweep reports its
  blocked scripts. Read `headers()` in the root layout so pages render per request. Preserve Payload
  file handlers' own SVG policy on `/api/<collection>/file/*`.
- Access boundary asks Payload's `/api/access` what an anonymous caller may do and compares the result
  with the project's `publicAccess` declarations, including field-level grants.

The browser sweep compares discovered route files with visited URLs and project test evidence.
Unlinked, authenticated, and dynamic routes need reachable links, a sitemap entry, or explicit project
scans of both kinds. A dynamic family requires a concrete member URL; its parent route is not a substitute.
Intentional exclusions require a reason. Do not narrow coverage merely to fit the route budget.

Some states no crawl reaches: signed-in and permission-restricted views, empty lists, error states, and
date-dependent variants. They are the project's responsibility. Cover a presentation component's state
with a Storybook story, and a real state with a project test that reaches it with seeded data and a real
session, never a mock, then calls `expectSweptPage(page)` from `ploaness/a11y`. It runs axe at 390 and
1280 and the layout, text and media rules at every baseline and declared viewport, in place, so one call
is evidence of both:

```ts
await page.goto(`/en/teams/${team.slug}`)
await expectSweptPage(page)
```

`expectNoLayoutDefects(page)` runs the layout, text and media rules alone, beside a project's own axe
scan. Both judge the page in the state the spec left it, resized rather than reloaded, so drive it into
the state worth judging first. They do not fix the browser clock or block third-party requests; the
sweep does.

Every Playwright test asserts. `expect`, the `ploaness/a11y` and `ploaness/sweep` helpers, and a project
helper named `expect` followed by a capital letter count as assertions; name an asserting helper that way.

Static route/test matching recognizes route literals, regular expressions, dynamic URL templates, and
axe and layout evidence through one relative helper import. It does not prove the spec executes those
scans. A route scanned with axe alone fails as `route-unmeasured`. Keep navigation and scans clear, run
the full browser suite, and account for URL changes introduced by `basePath`, redirects, rewrites,
proxy logic, or custom page extensions.

Use `expectSecurityHeaders(response)` from `ploaness/security` on the actual navigation or request response
in authenticated project tests. The assertions preserve the baseline decisions; they do not validate every
CSP interaction. Unvisited routes are not covered. Test project-specific file and API boundaries directly.
Static Next.js Web Vitals rules and the bundle budget cover deterministic
causes of performance problems; the harness does not claim a measured Lighthouse or Web Vitals score.

Pass CMS-supplied link values through `safeHref` from `@ploaness/runtime` before assigning an `href`.
Text escaping does not make an arbitrary URL scheme safe. Application code owns this call-site decision;
tests may also import the re-export from `ploaness/runtime`.

### Browser Sweep

Discovery runs first, once, in Chromium, as a setup project every pass depends on. It records what to
scan, and its findings and problems - the seed did not answer, the route cap was reached, a declared page
nothing scans - rather than failing, so every pass still runs. Each pass then runs as four shards, one
test each, on parallel workers that take its pages from one shared queue, so a shard that meets slow pages
does not hold the pass while the others wait; each page is loaded fresh and judged alone, so the split
changes no verdict. The project's own specs run on one worker beside them. Each shard has a budget of an
equal share of the work discovery found and reports every defect it finds; one failing shard hides nothing
another reports. The summary runs last, whatever failed before it: it fails on discovery's findings and problems,
on a shard that recorded no report, and on exemptions no shard in that engine used. Each finding is one
line:

```text
[rule] /route @390x844 (chromium, resting): what is wrong: element "its text"
```

`story:<id>` names a story. Each shard writes its report under `test-results/ploaness-sweep/<engine>/`,
with a `progress.log` there that gains a line as each item finishes; the discovery record, the summary's
`report.json` and the `coverage.txt` it prints sit in `test-results/ploaness-sweep/`. Outlined failure
screenshots are attached to the failing test; they show where a finding is and are never compared or
committed.

| Pass | Engines | Viewports | Pages |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Discovery | Chromium, once | desktop | every route and story |
| Resting rules | Chromium | baseline and declared; for routes also both sides of every breakpoint that styles them | every route and story |
| Interaction states | Chromium | routes at 390x844 and 1280x800, stories at 1280x800; hover at 1280 | every route and story |
| Disclosures | Chromium | routes at 390x844 and 1280x800, stories at 1280x800 | every route and story |
| Text stress | Chromium | spacing at 390x844 and 1280x800; resize at 1280x800 | one route per template, one story per component |
| Width sweep | Chromium | 320 to 1920 px in 32 px steps, bisected | one route per template, one story per component |
| Dark scheme | Chromium | 390x844 and 1280x800 | pages whose stylesheets answer `prefers-color-scheme`; a `.dark` class alone is not followed |
| Engine | Firefox, WebKit | baseline | one route per template, one story per component |

The baseline is 390x844, 768x1024 and 1280x800; the width sweep covers overflow at every width between
320 and 1920. Each viewport is a fresh load. Breakpoints are read from every `@media` width condition in
the page's stylesheets, `px`, `rem` and `em`, legacy and range syntax. A template is the route file a URL
resolves to, and a story's template is its component; sampling one route does not prove its siblings equivalent, and the resting pass still
covers every route.

Every page is loaded still: reduced motion, animations and transitions at zero duration, a hidden caret,
locale `en-US`, timezone UTC, pixel ratio 1, the browser's `Date` fixed at `ploaness.clock` while its
timers and animations keep running, and every request outside the application and auxiliary origins
blocked. Each page is settled - fonts, every image decoded, a quiet element tree, finished animations,
cleared `aria-busy` regions - and measured twice.

| Rule | Fails when |
| --- | --- |
| `page-error` | an uncaught error, `console.error`, or hydration warning occurs during load or interaction |
| `failed-request` | a subresource answers 4xx or 5xx, fails, or is blocked as a third-party request |
| `broken-link` | a crawled internal link, or a sitemap entry, does not answer |
| `page-not-stable` | the page does not settle within 10 s, a visible busy region never clears, or geometry moves more than 1 px between two measurements |
| `overflow` | the document is wider than the viewport; names the outermost element past its edge |
| `text-overlap` | two elements' own visible text shares more than one pixel row, unless one contains the other; text a clipping or scrolling ancestor hides, or a positioned layer of an opaque colour or opaque-stop gradient covers, is not visible; text in a rotated or skewed subtree is not compared |
| `clipped-text` | `overflow: hidden` or `clip` cuts content; ellipsis or line clamp passes when `title` or `aria-label` holds the full text |
| `attached` | boxed siblings are closer than the minimum gap, 4 CSS pixels by default, measured on what their clipping and scrolling ancestors leave visible; boxes in a rotated or skewed subtree are not compared |
| `obscured` | something other than a control or its descendant sits at the control's centre; a hidden or inert control, or one with `pointer-events: none`, is offered to no pointer and is not judged |
| `focus-obscured` | a keyboard-focused element is entirely hidden by fixed or sticky content |
| `focus-visible` | the pixels of a keyboard-focused element and a 4 px ring do not change when it takes focus |
| `focus-order` | tabbing moves focus to an element entirely above the previous one in the same landmark, and not to the head of a column further along the line; dialogs, fixed and sticky content and visually hidden elements are not compared |
| `focus-contrast` | the focus change shows, but the pixels whose colour contrasts 3:1 with their unfocused colour cover less than a 1 px line along half the element's longer side (WCAG 1.4.11): a faint tint or glow, not an outline, ring, underline or strong fill |
| `disclosure-keyboard` | `Escape` does not close a popup or modal dialog, focus does not return to its control within a second, or focus leaves an open modal dialog |
| `text-spacing` | the WCAG 1.4.12 overrides cause a new overflow, overlap, or clip |
| `text-resize` | doubling every text size causes a new overflow, overlap, or clip, or text does not grow |
| `small-text` | rendered text is below 12 px |
| `font-load` | a `@font-face` family the page's text uses has no loaded face, or a face errored |
| `image-load` | a visible image is incomplete or has no natural width |
| `image-distorted` | an `object-fit: fill` image is rendered out of its natural proportion |
| `image-upscaled` | an image is displayed more than 10% larger than its source file's pixels, whatever width its `srcset` declares |
| `media-space` | an in-flow image, video, frame or embed takes a different box once loaded than before |
| `axe:<id>` | axe reports a violation, at 390 and 1280, in the resting, dark, disclosure, hover and focus states; an `aria-hidden-focus` element is cleared when focusing it moves focus at once to a perceivable element outside it, as a dialog or menu library's focus guard does |
| `pass-budget` | a pass runs out of its budget; the finding names the item it reached |

The geometry rules judge computed boxes and styles. Touching boxes compare boxed siblings: a background
unlike the one beneath, a shadow, or a border facing the neighbor. Wrappers that paint nothing and hold
no text are looked through. Stacked full-width sections, zero-size, hidden, `aria-hidden`, out-of-flow
elements, and two inline elements in one run of text are not compared. Closed `<details>` bodies are not
measured; opening the disclosure measures its body.

Repair a finding where its cause is. A design that breaks a rule on purpose takes an exemption naming
the rules it excuses, on the element or, except for `attached`, an ancestor:

```tsx
<div role="tablist" data-ploaness-layout="attached" data-ploaness-layout-reason="tabs join their panel">
```

Each exemption counts toward the suppression ceiling. A missing reason, an unknown rule name, and a rule
the exemption excused in no pass, viewport, engine or state fail the sweep. Page-level rules -
`page-error`, `failed-request`, `broken-link`, `page-not-stable`, `font-load` - and axe take no element
exemption. A false positive is a defect of the rule; report it upstream rather than exempting it.

### Change Scope

Delta verification sweeps only the routes and stories a change can affect, in Chromium. The change is
everything that differs from the merge-base of `HEAD` with the remote-tracking default branch of the
first remote (`origin/main` when `origin/HEAD` is not recorded): commits not on it, staged and unstaged
edits, and untracked files that are not ignored. Local `main` is never the baseline, so unpushed commits
on it are checked. Nothing is fetched and nothing is remembered between runs; a stale `origin/main` only
widens the change. CI must check out with the default branch present, for example `fetch-depth: 0`.

A changed module reaches a route when the route's page, or a layout, template, loading, error or
not-found module above it, imports it directly or transitively, and a story when its story file does,
through the import graph the `arch` gate's analyzer resolves. A crawled route no file declares, and a
story outside the application, are swept. Discovery still crawls every route, so broken links and
security headers are judged everywhere.

The sweep covers everything, and says why, when there is no baseline (no remote-tracking default branch,
a shallow clone, no merge-base) or when a changed file is a global input: the lockfile, the workspace
file, `.npmrc`, `.nvmrc`, a manifest, `tsconfig.json`, the Next.js, Tailwind or PostCSS configuration,
middleware, proxy or instrumentation, an environment file, anything under `public/`, the Storybook
configuration, a stylesheet that is not a CSS module, the root layout, a file under a source root that
the import graph does not hold, or any file of a member the application starts as an auxiliary server
or depends on. Documentation, workflows, tests and tool configuration outside the source roots change
no page.

The scope follows imports, not data: content a seed script or a migration writes reaches the pages that
show it only through full verification. Full verification of the default branch is what makes a scoped
run safe.

`pnpm exec ploaness scope` prints the scope of the application it runs in without running anything, and
`--json` prints the document the `e2e` gate hands the sweep. `coverage.txt` begins with the same scope:
the level, the baseline commit, every changed file, each swept route and story with its reason, and how
many were not reached. A scoped pass is reported as scoped, never as complete coverage. `ploaness init`
writes `.github/workflows/verify-full.yml`, which runs full verification nightly and on release tags;
it is the project's from then on, so a project verifying elsewhere replaces it.
